{title: As with Gladness Men of Old}
{artist: William Dix & Conrad Kocher}
{key: G}
{time: 4/4}
{tempo: 88}

Verse 1
[G]As with gladness [C]men of [G]old [C]did the [G]guiding [D]star be[G]hold,
as with joy they [C]hailed its [G]light, [C]leading [G]onward, [D]beaming [G]bright —
so, most gracious [D]Lord, may [Em]we [C]ever[G]more be [D]led to [G]thee.

Verse 2
[G]As with joyful [C]steps they [G]sped [C]To that [G]lowly [D]manger bed
There to bend the [C]knee before [G]Him [C]whom [G]Heav'n [D]and [G]earth adore;
So may we [D]with willing [Em]feet [C]Ever seek [D]Thy mercy [G]seat.

Verse 3
[G]As they offered [C]gifts most [G]rare At [C]that manger [G]rude [D]and bare;
So may we with [C]holy joy, [G]Pure [C]and [G]free [D]from [G]sin's alloy,
All our costliest [D]treasures bring, [Em]Christ, [C]to Thee, [D]our heav'nly [G]King.

Verse 4
[G]Holy Jesus, ev'ry [C]day Keep [G]us in [C]the narrow [G]way; [D]And, when
earthly things are past, [C]Bring our [G]ransomed [C]souls [G]at [D]last [G]Where they
need no star [D]to guide, [Em]Where [C]no clouds [D]Thy glory [G]hide.

Verse 5
[G]In the heav'nly [C]country bright, [G]Need [C]they no [G]created [D]light; Thou
its Light, its Joy, [C]its Crown, [C]Thou [G]its [D]Sun [G]which goes
not down; There [D]forever may [Em]we [C]sing Alleluias [D]to our [G]King!
